<DocsLayout title="How it works" current="/docs/how-it-works">
  <p>
    TinyCPU is a 32-bit RISC-V (RV32I) processor written in SystemVerilog. It has two cores that share one
    instruction set: a single-cycle reference core, which finishes one instruction per clock, and a 5-stage
    pipelined core, which overlaps instructions and handles the hazards that creates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything on this site runs the real hardware. The SystemVerilog is compiled by Verilator into C++, the C++
    is compiled by Emscripten into WebAssembly, and the browser steps that WebAssembly one clock edge at a time.
    Nothing is simulated in JavaScript.
  </p>

  <h2>The chain, from source to screen</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>RTL</b>: <code>rtl/*.sv</code>, both cores and the memory-mapped I/O.</li>
    <li><b>Verilator</b>: turns the RTL into one C++ model per core, so both run in one WebAssembly module.</li>
    <li><b>Emscripten</b>: links the models into <code>tiny_cpu.wasm</code>, about 77 KB gzipped.</li>
    <li><b>The app</b>: an assembler and a viewer that show every cycle, every register and every byte of memory.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it: a program on the single-cycle core</h2>
  <p>
    The program below prints a greeting one byte at a time through the console register, then reports PASS.
    On the single-cycle core every instruction takes exactly one cycle, so the cycle count equals the instruction
    count. Step through it.
  </p>
  <MiniRun client:visible source={hello} core="SINGLE" caption="Hello, on the single-cycle core" />

